          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.108,
            0.887,
            0.19
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. A rally starts with 120 members. Each hour, 9 members drop out of the group. How many members will remain after 1, 2, 3, … hours? Find a linear expression to represent the number of members at the end of the n"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "th",
              "styles": [
                "superscript"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " hour."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.194,
            0.886,
            0.256
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Suppose the length of a rectangle is 13 cm. Find the area if the breadth is (i) 12 cm, (ii) 10 cm, (iii) 8 cm. Find the linear pattern representing the area of the rectangle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.259,
            0.886,
            0.341
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Suppose the length of a rectangular box is 7 cm and breadth is 11 cm. Find the volume if the height is (i) 5 cm, (ii) 9 cm, (iii) 13 cm. Find the linear pattern representing the volume of the rectangular box."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.345,
            0.886,
            0.408
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Sarita is reading a book of 500 pages. She reads 20 pages every day. How many pages will be left after 15 days? Express this as a linear pattern."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.423,
            0.649,
            0.446
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2.4. Linear growth and linear decay"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.456,
            0.885,
            0.497
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Linear expressions help to model situations or phenomena where there is growth or decline. Consider the following examples:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.498,
            0.886,
            0.577
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 9:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The cost of a journey is given by the linear function "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "C(d) = 100 + 60d"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " where C indicates total cost in rupees and d the distance travelled in km. Let us make a table of values for d varying from 0 to 10 km and show how the cost increases for every km."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.587,
            0.878,
            0.644
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.117,
                0.587,
                0.878,
                0.644
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_8_table_9.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tbody><tr><td>Distance travelled, d (km)</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>Cost, C (`)</td><td>100</td><td>160</td><td>220</td><td>280</td><td>340</td><td>400</td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.659,
            0.885,
            0.72
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In this example, as the value of d increases by one km, the value of the cost function C, increases by a fixed amount of `60. This is an example of "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "linear growth.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.742,
            0.399,
            0.766
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.778,
            0.868,
            0.819
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What is the cost for travelling 15 km? For how many kilometres will the cost of the journey be `700?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.835,
            0.886,
            0.895
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 10:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The height of water in a cylindrical tank is 3 m at the start of summer. The height h m at the end of t months is given by the linear function h(t) = 3 – 0.5t."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
